WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:02.920
Now let's have a quick look to the gold layer specifications.

00:02.920 --> 00:04.120
So this is the final stage.

00:04.120 --> 00:07.840
We can provide data to be consumed by reporting and analytics.

00:07.840 --> 00:10.200
And this time we will not be building tables.

00:10.200 --> 00:11.720
We will be using views.

00:11.720 --> 00:17.080
So that means we will not be having like stored procedure or any load process to the gold layer.

00:17.120 --> 00:19.920
All what we are doing is only data transformation.

00:19.920 --> 00:24.560
And the focus of the data transformation is going to be data integration, aggregation, business logic

00:24.560 --> 00:25.160
and so on.

00:25.160 --> 00:27.880
And this time we are going to introduce a new data model.

00:27.880 --> 00:29.880
We will be doing star schema.

00:29.880 --> 00:32.520
So those are the specifications for the gold layer.

00:32.520 --> 00:34.040
And this is our scope.

00:34.040 --> 00:39.320
So this time we make sure that we are selecting data from the silver layer not from the bronze.

00:39.360 --> 00:44.960
Because the bronze has bad data quality and the silver is everything is prepared and cleaned up in order

00:44.960 --> 00:48.400
to build the gold layer going to be targeting the silver layer.

00:48.440 --> 00:55.120
So let's start with select star from and we're going to go to the silver CRM customer info.

00:55.120 --> 00:57.000
So let's go and hit execute.

00:57.000 --> 01:01.800
And now we're going to go and select the columns that we need to be presented in the gold layer.

01:01.840 --> 01:04.410
So let's start selecting the columns that we want.

01:04.450 --> 01:07.610
We have the ID the key, the first name.

01:12.210 --> 01:14.530
I will not go and get the metadata information.

01:14.530 --> 01:16.130
This only belongs to the silver.

01:16.130 --> 01:16.610
Perfect.

01:16.610 --> 01:19.410
The next step is that I'm going to go and give this table an alias.

01:19.410 --> 01:20.930
So let's go and call it CI.

01:21.130 --> 01:25.130
And I'm going to make sure that we are selecting from this alias.

01:25.130 --> 01:28.770
Because later we're going to go and join this table with other tables.

01:28.770 --> 01:29.850
So something like this.

01:29.890 --> 01:31.330
So we're going to go with those columns.

01:31.370 --> 01:33.090
Now let's move to the second table.

01:33.130 --> 01:35.490
Let's go and get the birthday information.

01:35.490 --> 01:37.370
So now we're going to jump to the other system.

01:37.370 --> 01:41.690
And we have to join the data by the CID together with the customer key.

01:41.690 --> 01:44.530
So now we have to go and join the data with another table.

01:44.530 --> 01:50.890
And here I try to avoid using the inner join, because if the other table doesn't have all the information

01:50.890 --> 01:53.570
about the customers, I might lose customers.

01:53.570 --> 01:59.530
So always start with the master table, and if you join it with any other table in order to get information,

01:59.530 --> 02:05.210
try always to avoid the inner join because the other source might not have all the customers, and if

02:05.210 --> 02:07.050
you do inner join, you might lose customers.

02:07.050 --> 02:12.370
So I tend to start from the master table and then everything else is about the left join.

02:12.370 --> 02:16.970
So I'm going to say left join silver ERP customer A-z12.

02:17.010 --> 02:18.650
So let's give it the alias CA.

02:18.770 --> 02:20.370
And now we have to join the tables.

02:20.370 --> 02:23.610
So it's going to be by see from the first table.

02:23.610 --> 02:26.770
It's going to be the customer key equal to CA.

02:26.970 --> 02:29.010
And we have the CID.

02:29.050 --> 02:32.610
Now of course we're going to get matching data because we checked the silver layer.

02:32.610 --> 02:37.410
But if we haven't prepared the data and the silver layer we have to do here preparation step in order

02:37.410 --> 02:38.330
to join the tables.

02:38.330 --> 02:42.330
But we don't have to do that because that was a pre step in the silver layer.

02:42.330 --> 02:46.450
So now you can see the systematic that we have in this bronze silver gold.

02:46.490 --> 02:51.290
So now after joining the tables we have to go and pick the information that we need from the second

02:51.370 --> 02:53.410
table which is the birth date.

02:53.410 --> 02:54.450
So be date.

02:54.450 --> 02:58.050
And as well from this table there is another nice information.

02:58.210 --> 03:00.690
It is the gender information.

03:00.690 --> 03:03.610
So that's all what we need from the second table.

03:03.610 --> 03:05.500
Let's go and check the third table.

03:05.500 --> 03:09.340
So the third table is about the location information, the countries.

03:09.340 --> 03:12.740
And as well we connect the tables by the CID with the key.

03:12.780 --> 03:14.180
So let's go and do that.

03:14.180 --> 03:19.220
We're going to say as well left join silver ERP location.

03:19.220 --> 03:20.860
And I'm going to give it the name LA.

03:21.020 --> 03:22.060
And then we have to join.

03:22.060 --> 03:22.980
Why the keys.

03:23.020 --> 03:23.780
The same thing.

03:23.820 --> 03:28.860
It's going to be CI customer key equal to La CID.

03:28.940 --> 03:32.500
Again we have prepared those IDs and keys in the server layer.

03:32.500 --> 03:34.140
So the join should be working.

03:34.140 --> 03:37.180
Now we have to go and pick the data from the second table.

03:37.180 --> 03:38.340
So what do we have over here.

03:38.340 --> 03:41.340
We have the ID the country and the metadata information.

03:41.340 --> 03:44.220
So let's go and just get the country perfect.

03:44.220 --> 03:50.180
So now with that we have joined all the three tables and we have picked all the columns that we want

03:50.220 --> 03:51.020
in this object.

03:51.020 --> 03:55.100
So again by looking over here we have joined this table with this one and this one.

03:55.100 --> 04:01.140
So with that we have collected all that customer informations that we have from the two source systems.

04:01.180 --> 04:01.540
Okay.

04:01.580 --> 04:05.020
So now let's go and query in order to make sure that we have everything correct.

04:05.020 --> 04:10.780
And in order to understand that your joints are correct, you have to keep your eye on those three columns.

04:10.780 --> 04:16.500
So if you are seeing that you are getting data, that means you are doing the joints correctly.

04:16.500 --> 04:22.260
But if you are seeing a lot of nulls or no data at all, that means your joints are incorrect.

04:22.260 --> 04:24.780
But now it looks for me it is working.

04:24.780 --> 04:30.380
And another check that I do is that if your first table has no duplicates, what could happen is that

04:30.380 --> 04:35.900
after doing multiple joints, you might now start getting duplicates because the relationship between

04:35.900 --> 04:37.460
those tables is not clear.

04:37.460 --> 04:42.020
1 to 1 you might get like one to many relationship or many to many relationships.

04:42.020 --> 04:47.580
So now the check that I usually do at this stage, that I have to make sure that I don't have duplicates

04:47.580 --> 04:48.780
from the results.

04:48.780 --> 04:52.020
So we don't have like multiple rows for the same customer.

04:52.020 --> 04:55.220
So in order to do that, we go and do a quick groupby.

04:55.660 --> 05:03.140
So we're going to group by the data, by the customer ID, and then we do the counts from this subquery.

05:03.140 --> 05:05.300
So this is the whole subquery.

05:05.540 --> 05:14.110
And then after that we're going to go and say group by the customer ID and then we say having counts

05:15.270 --> 05:16.510
higher than one.

05:16.510 --> 05:22.390
So this query actually tried to find out whether we have any duplicates in the primary key.

05:22.390 --> 05:24.190
So let's go and execute it.

05:24.230 --> 05:25.790
We don't have any duplicates.

05:25.790 --> 05:32.350
And that means after joining all those tables with their customer info, those tables didn't cause any

05:32.350 --> 05:34.430
issues and they didn't duplicate my data.

05:34.430 --> 05:35.830
So this is very important.

05:35.830 --> 05:38.630
Check to make sure that you are in the right way.

05:38.670 --> 05:39.030
All right.

05:39.030 --> 05:42.070
So that means everything is fine about the duplicates.

05:42.070 --> 05:43.830
We don't have to worry about it.

05:43.870 --> 05:45.790
Now we have here an integration issue.

05:45.790 --> 05:47.270
So let's go and execute it again.

05:47.310 --> 05:50.950
And now if you look to the data we have two sources for the gender information.

05:50.950 --> 05:54.630
One comes from the CRM and another one comes from the ERP.

05:54.670 --> 05:56.830
So now the question is what are we going to do with this.

05:56.870 --> 05:59.390
Well we have to do data integration.

05:59.390 --> 06:01.030
So let me show you how I do it.

06:01.030 --> 06:03.230
First I go and have a new query.

06:03.230 --> 06:06.190
And then I'm going to go and remove all other stuff.

06:06.190 --> 06:11.590
And I'm going to leave only those two informations Formations and use a distinct just to focus on the

06:11.590 --> 06:13.990
integration and let's go and execute it.

06:13.990 --> 06:16.110
And maybe as well to do an order by.

06:16.150 --> 06:17.830
So let's do one and two.

06:17.870 --> 06:19.230
Let's go and execute it again.

06:19.230 --> 06:21.390
So now here we have all the scenarios.

06:21.390 --> 06:23.670
And we can see sometimes there is a matching.

06:23.670 --> 06:27.390
So from the first table we have female and the other table we have as well female.

06:27.430 --> 06:32.190
But sometimes we have an issue like those two tables are giving different informations and the same

06:32.190 --> 06:33.110
thing over here.

06:33.110 --> 06:35.390
So this is as well an issue different informations.

06:35.390 --> 06:40.710
Another scenario where we have a data from the first table like here we have the female but in the other

06:40.710 --> 06:42.550
table we have not available.

06:42.550 --> 06:44.030
Well this is not a problem.

06:44.030 --> 06:45.910
So we can get it from the first table.

06:45.910 --> 06:51.270
But we have as well the exact opposite scenario where from the first table the data is not available,

06:51.270 --> 06:53.550
but it is available from the second table.

06:53.590 --> 06:56.910
And now here you might wonder why I'm getting a null over here.

06:56.910 --> 07:01.870
We did handle all the missing data in the silver layer and we replace everything with not available.

07:01.870 --> 07:03.630
So why we are still getting a null?

07:03.670 --> 07:09.590
This null doesn't come directly from the tables, it just comes because of joining tables.

07:09.590 --> 07:16.480
So that means there are customers in the CRM table that is not available in the Erb table.

07:16.480 --> 07:19.080
And if there is like no match, what's going to happen?

07:19.080 --> 07:20.880
We will get a null from SQL.

07:20.880 --> 07:23.840
So this null means there was no match.

07:23.880 --> 07:25.680
And that's why we are getting this null.

07:25.680 --> 07:28.200
It is not coming from the content of the tables.

07:28.200 --> 07:29.920
And this is of course an issue.

07:29.920 --> 07:33.240
But now the big issue what can happen for those two scenarios.

07:33.240 --> 07:35.640
Here we have the data but they are different.

07:35.640 --> 07:38.360
And here again we have to ask the experts about it.

07:38.400 --> 07:40.120
What is the master here?

07:40.120 --> 07:42.680
Is it the CRM system or the ERP.

07:42.720 --> 07:48.400
And let's say from their answer going to say the master data for the customer information is the CRM.

07:48.600 --> 07:54.480
So that means the CRM informations are more accurate than the Erb information.

07:54.480 --> 07:56.600
And this is only about the customers of course.

07:56.600 --> 08:02.120
So for this scenario where we have female and male, then the correct information is the female from

08:02.120 --> 08:03.160
the first source system.

08:03.160 --> 08:04.400
The same goes over here.

08:04.400 --> 08:06.160
And here we have like a male and female.

08:06.160 --> 08:11.040
Then the correct one is the male because this source system is the master.

08:11.080 --> 08:11.280
Okay.

08:11.320 --> 08:13.610
So now let's go and build this business rule.

08:13.610 --> 08:15.330
We're going to start, as usual with the case.

08:15.690 --> 08:22.170
So the first very important rule is if we have a data in the gender information from the CRM system

08:22.170 --> 08:24.410
from the master, then go and use it.

08:24.410 --> 08:27.890
So we're going to go and check the gender information from the CRM table.

08:28.050 --> 08:32.010
So customer gender is not equal to not available.

08:32.010 --> 08:34.770
So that means we have a value male or female.

08:34.810 --> 08:37.050
Let me just have here a comma like this.

08:37.050 --> 08:38.490
Then what can happen.

08:38.530 --> 08:39.450
Go and use it.

08:39.450 --> 08:47.570
So we're going to use the value from the master CRM is the master for gender info.

08:47.610 --> 08:47.890
Now.

08:47.930 --> 08:52.890
Otherwise that means it is not available from the CRM table.

08:53.170 --> 08:56.850
Then go and use and grab the information from the second table.

08:56.850 --> 08:59.650
So we're going to say CA gender.

08:59.650 --> 09:02.290
But now we have to be careful with this null over here.

09:02.290 --> 09:04.690
We have to convert it to not available as well.

09:04.690 --> 09:06.290
So we're going to use the coalesce.

09:07.330 --> 09:12.010
So if this is a null then go and use the not available like this.

09:12.370 --> 09:13.370
So that's it.

09:13.370 --> 09:14.410
Let's have an end.

09:14.450 --> 09:16.490
Let me just push this over here.

09:16.490 --> 09:19.810
So let's go and call it New Gen for now.

09:19.850 --> 09:21.050
Let's go and execute it.

09:21.050 --> 09:23.250
And let's go and check the different scenarios.

09:23.250 --> 09:25.370
All those values over here.

09:25.370 --> 09:27.690
We have data from the CRM system.

09:27.690 --> 09:30.650
And this is as well represented in the new column.

09:30.650 --> 09:34.650
But now for the second part we don't have data from the first system.

09:34.650 --> 09:37.490
So we are trying to get it from the second system.

09:37.490 --> 09:39.570
So for the first one is not available.

09:39.570 --> 09:42.050
And then we try to get it from the second source system.

09:42.050 --> 09:43.810
So now we are activating the else.

09:43.850 --> 09:45.010
Well it is null.

09:45.010 --> 09:47.450
And with that the coalesce is activated.

09:47.450 --> 09:52.010
And we are replacing the null with not available for the second scenario as well.

09:52.010 --> 09:55.410
The first system don't have the gender information.

09:55.410 --> 09:57.770
That's why we are grabbing it from the second.

09:57.770 --> 10:01.050
So with that we have a female and then the third one the same thing.

10:01.090 --> 10:04.130
We don't have information, but we get it from the second source system.

10:04.130 --> 10:06.010
We have the male and the last one.

10:06.010 --> 10:08.610
It is not available in both source systems.

10:08.610 --> 10:10.570
That's why we are getting not available.

10:10.570 --> 10:16.020
So with that, as you can see, we have a perfect new column where we are integrating two different

10:16.020 --> 10:17.340
source systems in one.

10:17.340 --> 10:20.340
And this is exactly what we call data integration.

10:20.340 --> 10:24.860
This piece of information, it is way better than the source CRM.

10:24.860 --> 10:29.460
And as with the source ERP, it is more rich and has more information.

10:29.460 --> 10:35.380
And this is exactly why we tried to get data from different source system in order to get rich information

10:35.380 --> 10:38.420
in the data warehouse, so that we have a nice logic.

10:38.420 --> 10:43.420
And as you can see, it's way easier to separate it and separate query in order first to build the logic

10:43.420 --> 10:46.060
and then take it to the original query.

10:46.060 --> 10:50.420
So what I'm going to do, I'm just going to go and copy everything from here and go back to our query.

10:50.420 --> 10:53.980
I'm going to go and delete those informations that gender.

10:54.020 --> 10:56.980
And I will put our new logic over here.

10:56.980 --> 11:01.460
So a comma and let's go and execute so that we have our new nice column.

11:01.460 --> 11:03.300
Now with that we have very nice objects.

11:03.300 --> 11:06.220
We don't have duplicates and we have integrated data together.

11:06.220 --> 11:09.420
So we took three tables and we put it in one object.

11:09.460 --> 11:13.780
Now the next step is that we're going to go and give nice friendly names.

11:13.780 --> 11:19.180
The rule in the gold layer that to use friendly names and not to follow the names that we get from the

11:19.180 --> 11:19.820
source system.

11:19.820 --> 11:23.660
And we have to make sure that we are following the rules by the naming conventions.

11:23.660 --> 11:25.780
So we are following the snakecase.

11:25.780 --> 11:27.580
So let's go and do it step by step.

11:27.580 --> 11:28.500
For the first one.

11:28.500 --> 11:30.660
Let's go and call it the customer ID.

11:30.700 --> 11:34.500
And then the next one I will get rid of using keys and so on.

11:34.500 --> 11:39.540
I'm going to go and call it customer number because those are customer numbers.

11:39.580 --> 11:45.740
Then for the next one we're going to call it first name without using any prefixes.

11:45.980 --> 11:48.540
And the next one last name.

11:48.700 --> 11:51.900
And we have here marital status.

11:52.380 --> 11:55.540
So I will be using the exact name but without the prefix.

11:55.540 --> 11:58.060
And here we are just going to call it gender.

11:58.060 --> 12:01.460
And this one we're going to call it create date.

12:01.460 --> 12:04.180
And this one birth date.

12:04.180 --> 12:06.660
And the last one is going to be the country.

12:07.060 --> 12:09.140
So let's go and execute it.

12:09.180 --> 12:11.700
Now as you can see the names are really friendly.

12:11.700 --> 12:16.740
So we have customer ID customer numbers first name last name status gender.

12:16.830 --> 12:20.630
So as you can see, the names are really nice and really easy to understand.

12:20.670 --> 12:23.910
Now the next tip I'm going to think about the order of those columns.

12:23.910 --> 12:24.550
So the first two.

12:24.590 --> 12:26.110
It makes sense to have it together.

12:26.110 --> 12:27.510
The first name, last name.

12:27.510 --> 12:30.030
Then I think the country is very important information.

12:30.030 --> 12:35.470
So I'm gonna go and get it from here and put it exactly after the last name is just nicer.

12:35.470 --> 12:36.990
So let's go and execute it again.

12:36.990 --> 12:38.990
So the first name last name country.

12:38.990 --> 12:41.670
It's always nice to group up relevant columns together.

12:41.670 --> 12:42.030
Right.

12:42.030 --> 12:44.790
So we have here the status of the gender and so on.

12:44.790 --> 12:46.910
And then we have the create date and the birth date.

12:46.910 --> 12:49.030
I think I'm going to go and switch the birth date.

12:49.030 --> 12:52.670
With the create date it's more important than the create date.

12:52.710 --> 12:53.510
Like this.

12:53.750 --> 12:55.230
And here not forget a comma.

12:55.230 --> 12:56.310
So execute again.

12:56.350 --> 12:58.070
So it looks wonderful.

12:58.110 --> 13:01.230
Now comes a very important decision about these objects.

13:01.270 --> 13:03.510
Is it a fact table or a dimension.

13:03.510 --> 13:08.310
Well as we learned dimensions hold descriptive informations about an object.

13:08.310 --> 13:12.270
And as you can see we have here a descriptions about the customers.

13:12.270 --> 13:15.670
So all those columns are describing their customer information.

13:15.670 --> 13:20.510
And we don't have here like transactions and events and we don't have like measures and so on.

13:20.510 --> 13:23.310
So we cannot say this object is effect.

13:23.310 --> 13:25.150
It is clearly a dimension.

13:25.150 --> 13:29.230
So that's why we're going to go and call this object the dimension customer.

13:29.270 --> 13:34.590
Now there is one thing that if you are creating a new dimension, you need always a primary key for

13:34.590 --> 13:35.430
the dimension.

13:35.470 --> 13:40.070
Of course, we can go over here and depend on the primary key that we get from the source system.

13:40.270 --> 13:45.110
But sometimes you can have like dimensions where you don't have like a primary key that you can count

13:45.110 --> 13:45.590
on.

13:45.590 --> 13:50.550
So what we have to do is to go and generate a new primary key in the data warehouse.

13:50.550 --> 13:53.470
And those primary keys, we call it surrogate keys.

13:53.510 --> 14:00.590
Surrogate keys are system generated unique identifier that is assigned to each record to make the record

14:00.590 --> 14:01.030
unique.

14:01.070 --> 14:02.470
It is not a business key.

14:02.510 --> 14:05.990
It has no meaning and no one in the business knows about it.

14:06.030 --> 14:12.070
We only use it in order to connect our data model, and in this way, we have more control on how to

14:12.070 --> 14:13.390
connect our data model.

14:13.390 --> 14:16.790
And we don't have to depend always on the source system.

14:16.790 --> 14:22.640
And there are different ways on how to generate surrogate keys, like defining it in the DDL, or maybe

14:22.640 --> 14:25.640
using the window function row number and this data warehouse.

14:25.640 --> 14:29.640
I'm going to go with a simple solution where we're going to go and use the window function.

14:29.640 --> 14:35.080
So now in order to generate a surrogate key for this dimension, what we're going to do is very simple.

14:35.080 --> 14:38.360
So we're going to say row number over.

14:38.840 --> 14:41.880
And here we have to order by something.

14:41.880 --> 14:47.520
You can order by the create date or the customer ID or the customer number, whatever you want.

14:47.520 --> 14:51.440
But in this example I'm going to go and order by the customer ID.

14:52.120 --> 14:57.560
So we have to follow the naming convention that all surrogate keys with the key at the end as a suffix.

14:57.560 --> 14:59.840
So now let's go and query those informations.

14:59.840 --> 15:02.680
And as you can see at the start we have a customer key.

15:02.680 --> 15:06.520
And this is a sequence we don't have here of course any duplicates.

15:06.560 --> 15:10.240
And now this surrogate key is generated in the data warehouse.

15:10.240 --> 15:13.680
And we can use this key in order to connect the data model.

15:13.680 --> 15:15.840
So now with that our query is ready.

15:16.000 --> 15:19.480
And the last step is that we're going to go and create the objects.

15:19.480 --> 15:23.680
And as we decided all the objects in the gold layer are going to be a virtual one.

15:23.680 --> 15:25.960
So that means we're going to go and create a view.

15:25.960 --> 15:30.200
So we're going to say create view gold dot dim.

15:30.360 --> 15:31.960
So follow the naming convention.

15:32.000 --> 15:33.280
Start for the dimension.

15:33.280 --> 15:35.120
And we're going to have the customers.

